/// <reference types="@figma/plugin-typings" />

// Runtime data-access harness (#3826). Drives EVERY `REGISTRY` builder
// (`src/registry.ts`) against the real, committed `src/data/components.json`
// — outside Figma, no sandbox, no `@figma/plugin-typings` runtime, just the
// actual builder code + the actual JSON.
//
// ─── Why this exists — the axis no other guard covers ───────────────────
//
// G1 (regen-diff) checks Dart → JSON freshness. G6 (`tsc --noEmit`) checks
// that a builder's access *expressions* type-check against the JSON's
// inferred shape. G7 (literal ratchet) counts hardcoded chrome that hasn't
// been converted to read from the JSON yet. None of the three can see
// whether a value a builder reads *actually arrives* at a Figma binding
// call — that's a runtime fact, not a static one.
//
// This project was bitten by exactly that gap once: the schema v2
// migration left `data.ts` importing the JSON through
// `as unknown as ComponentsDoc` — a double cast that discards ALL
// structural checking, so `tsc` stayed green while every builder that
// touched `data.ts` threw a `TypeError` the moment it actually ran. The
// cast is gone (`resolveJsonModule` infers the shape directly, no
// interface to fall out of sync — see `src/data.ts`'s own header comment),
// but "the cast is gone" and "a value reaches every binding call" are two
// different claims. This harness is the only thing in the repo that checks
// the second one, by actually calling every builder.
//
// ─── The double — figma/VarLookup ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `data.ts`/`compat/v1.ts` (the adapters that turn JSON leaves into the
// shapes `helpers.ts` consumes) are NOT stubbed — real code, real JSON,
// unmodified. Only two things are doubled, and both record every call they
// receive:
//
//   1. `figma.*` — creates plain JS node objects (no Figma sandbox exists
//      outside the plugin runtime) that support exactly the node API the
//      101 builders + helpers.ts actually call (grep-verified — see the
//      PR description for the exact surface). Anything a builder calls
//      that isn't implemented here throws a plain `TypeError` — which is
//      itself a correct "this builder does something the double doesn't
//      model yet" failure, attributed to that builder below.
//
//   2. `VarLookup` (subclassed, not reimplemented — see `FakeVarLookup`
//      below) — every binding method (`.sem`/`.space`/`.radius`/`.size`/
//      `.sizeOrNull`/`.stroke`/`.fontSize`/`.fontFamily`/`.fontFamilyName`)
//      throws THE INSTANT it receives `undefined`/`NaN`/an empty string
//      instead of a real value. This is the actual net: it doesn't
//      validate against a real token catalog (this harness has no access
//      to one, and doesn't need one) — it validates that whatever
//      `data.ts`/`compat/v1.ts` handed the builder was a real, present
//      value by the time it reached the point Figma would bind it.
//
// This also catches the quieter failure mode the regen-diff/tsc/ratchet
// trio structurally cannot: a leaf-shape mismatch (e.g. a `ColorData.color`
// consumer fed a v2 `ref` instead) doesn't normally throw at all —
// `fillData` just paints a raw literal instead of binding a Variable, and
// `borderData` silently skips the border. Nothing about that is visibly
// wrong until a designer opens the file. But the *specific* shape drift
// this harness targets — a dimensional/color leaf missing the field an
// adapter expects — usually degrades to `undefined` reaching a binding
// call somewhere downstream, which is exactly what throws here.
//
// ─── Running it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `npm run selftest:registry-runtime` (folded into `npm run selftest`, so
// CI's existing G6 step — `npm ci && npm run typecheck && npm run
// selftest` — picks this up with no separate wiring). Bundled by esbuild
// (transpile-only, matching the other two selftest scripts) and run under
// plain Node — no `@types/node`, so `process` is declared locally like the
// existing selftest scripts do.

